(defmodule playground-core
  "The **live-evaluating buffer**, as a mechanism — everything about a playground that is
not about a language.

`playground` (Brood) and `elixir-playground` are the same feature twice: a free-text
buffer whose top-level forms evaluate as you settle typing, each one's result as ghost
text at its end, a pane beside it showing what the form under the cursor actually ran.
Every hard part of that is language-neutral and lives here:

  [[realign-evaluated]]  re-key what the session knows to the forms as they are NOW
  [[stale-forms]]        which forms an edit could have changed the answer of
  [[launch-plan]]        what to re-send, with the suffix rule as the safe fallback
  [[ed-launch]]          the debounce: parse, plan, send, stamp
  [[ed-reanchor]]        the keystroke: move the notes with the text, O(notes)
  [[sandbox-reply]]      one reply → this form's note and this form's cascade

What a LANGUAGE supplies is a spec (see below): how to split text into top-level forms,
how to tell mid-typing from broken, what a reply's note says, how to send one, and which
session to start. Symbols and sentences, resolved late, and nothing else.

## The three records a playground keeps, and why they are three

Everything here turns on not conflating them.

  `:playground-evaluated`  `{index -> {:src :deps}}` — what the SESSION has actually
                           evaluated. A launch does not write it; it CLEARS the entry of
                           every form it sends, and only a reply landing puts one back.
                           \"What we asked for\" written here as \"what happened\" is a bug
                           with a long tail: a reply dropped because the buffer moved on
                           left the launch's record claiming the session held that
                           definition, so the next launch saw nothing to re-send and the
                           session kept the OLD one, for as long as that form's text
                           stayed put.

  `:playground-notes`      `{index -> {:src :note}}` — what each form's answer SAYS,
                           beside the source it was stamped for, with no line in it.

  `:eol-notes`             the anchored, renderable projection of the above onto the text
                           as it stands. Rebuilt by every launch and every re-anchor.

Keeping the last two apart is what lets a re-anchor UN-PLACE a note — its form is
mid-edit, so it must not paint at the line that form used to occupy — without destroying
it: undo the edit and the projection puts it straight back. Fold them together and a
typo-then-backspace loses that form's result until you edit it again, because the launch
that would re-derive it never runs; the text is already what it last parsed.

## The spec

    {:key :elixir-playground         ; where this playground's state lives in the model
     :buffer \"*Elixir Playground*\"
     :spy {…}                        ; the `workings` pane spec
     :type :elixir-playground        ; the buffer type `register-type-layers` bound
     :timeout-ms 15000
     :restart \"M-x elixir-playground-restart\"
     :forms-of 'sym                  ; (text [tree]) -> positioned nodes, error-tolerant
     :parse-state 'sym               ; (src) -> :blank | :ok | [:incomplete r] | [:err m]
     :deps-of 'sym                   ; (text [tree]) -> per-form {:defines :references}, or ()
     :namespace-form? 'sym           ; (src) -> does this change what names mean below it?
     :note-of 'sym                   ; (reply src) -> the ghost-text note
     :eval 'sym                      ; (id src timeout-ms) -> was it accepted?
     :in-image 'sym                  ; (src) -> a reply map, or nil — the headless path
     :start 'sym                     ; (m) -> start (or re-root) the session
     :running? 'sym                  ; () -> is the session's worker alive?
     :reset 'sym                     ; () -> a fresh child
     :welcome \"…\"                    ; a new buffer's text
     :open-message \"…\"               ; or 'sym, (m booting?) -> what [[open]] says
     :restart-message \"…\"            ; what [[restart]] says
     :quiet-ms 1200                  ; optional: hold a NEW error while its form is typed
     :held? 'sym                     ; optional: (src) -> why a ready form waits for C-c C-c
     :spawn-failed-message \"…\"       ; optional: [[sandbox-down]]'s `:spawn-failed`
     :spy-faces [[test text role] …]} ; the pane's colouring — see [[spy-fontify]]

Symbols, resolved at call time, for the same reason a keymap names a command symbol: a
service redefined at runtime takes effect on the next keystroke. `:forms-of` and `:deps-of`
are handed the buffer's held tree (`syntaxtree`) as a second argument only when it has one
for exactly that text, so a language without trees keeps the one-argument form.

## What a client that is not a free-text buffer adds

The tutorial is the third client, and its \"forms\" are the ╭─ boxes of a lesson page: each
one carries lesson DATA (an expected answer, a hint, names to trace), some are prose that is
graded on its text and never sent, and a box's answer sits on the box's last form rather
than under it. Each of those is an optional key, absent for both playgrounds:

    {:form-data 'sym      ; (m) -> per-form data, by index; with it, `:parse-state`,
                          ; `:note-of` and `:eval` each take the form's datum LAST
     :note-line 'sym      ; (rope lo hi) -> the line a note for [lo hi) sits on
     :on-verdict 'sym     ; (m i note datum) -> m, after an ANSWER lands: a reply, the
                          ; in-image path, or a form answered locally (below)
     :after-launch 'sym   ; (m) -> m, the end of every launch
     :after-reply 'sym    ; (m) -> m, after a reply has landed
     :held-how \"…\"        ; what releases a held form here (`liveeval/held-note`)
     :down-says {…}}      ; [[sandbox-down]]'s sentences, over the defaults

A `:parse-state` may also answer `[:answered note]`: the language graded the form itself, with
no session involved — the note is stamped (nil says nothing) and nothing is sent."
  (:use model) ; ed-buffer-index-by-name / ed-buf-at / ed-update-named / ed-current-buf
  (:use pool) ; ed-select / ed-show-named — open / reselect the playground's buffer
  (:use editor/buffer) ; buffer-text / buffer-rope / annotate / buffer-marker
  (:use sandbox-events :only []) ; `register-client` registers there as a client loads
  (:use theme :only [*overlay0* *subtext* *teal*])) ; the palette the faces are drawn from
;; liveeval, workings, results, modes, theme and session (the ONE request-id
;; space every session mints from) are referenced qualified below — each `mod/name`
;; auto-loads its module.

;; ---- reading the spec ------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- svc (spec key args)
  "Call the spec's `key` service with `args`. Late resolution is the point — see the
module docstring."
  (apply (reflect/eval (get spec key)) args))

(defn- has? (spec key) (not (nil? (get spec key))))

(defn- form-data (spec m)
  ;; The per-form data the language keeps (`:form-data`), read once per launch or reply —
  ;; nil for a language that keeps none.
  (when (has? spec :form-data) (svc spec :form-data (list m))))

(defn- svc-form (spec key args data i)
  ;; A per-form service: `args`, plus form `i`'s datum when the language keeps any. Appended
  ;; only then, so a playground's services keep their plain arities.
  (svc spec key (if (has? spec :form-data) (append args (list (nth data i nil))) args)))

(defn- pg-hook (spec key m & args)
  ;; An optional `(m …) -> m` hook, or `m` untouched when the language has none.
  (if (has? spec key) (svc spec key (cons m args)) m))

(defn buffer-name (spec)
  "The name of the playground's buffer — the spec's `:buffer`."
  (get spec :buffer))

(defn pg-index (spec m)
  "Where this playground's buffer sits in the pool, or nil when it is not open."
  (ed-buffer-index-by-name m (buffer-name spec)))

(defn pg-buf (spec m)
  "This playground's buffer, or nil when it is not open."
  (let (i (pg-index spec m)) (when i (ed-buf-at m i))))

(defn- pg-held-tree (spec m b)
  ;; The tree the loop keeps for this playground's buffer (`syntaxtree`), when it was parsed
  ;; from exactly `b`'s text — nil for a buffer with none (Brood's playground and the
  ;; tutorial are not tree-sitter buffers; an Elixir one off screen has no tree yet)
  (let (index (pg-index spec m)
        lang (ed-mode-service b :ts-lang))
    (when (and index lang)
      (syntaxtree/held-tree-for (syntaxtree/syntax-tree-entry m index) b lang))))

(defn- pg-text-svc (spec key m b text)
  ;; A whole-buffer service (`:forms-of`, `:deps-of`) over `text`, handed the buffer's held
  ;; tree as a second argument when there is one, so a tree-sitter language reads its form
  ;; list off the tree the loop already keeps instead of parsing the buffer per launch. A
  ;; language without a tree keeps its plain `(text)` arity.
  (let (tree (pg-held-tree spec m b))
    (svc spec key (if tree (list text tree) (list text)))))

(defn- pg-update-buf (spec m f)
  ;; Apply `f` to this playground's buffer wherever it sits in the pool. Every
  ;; note-stamping path goes through here, so none of them has to care whether the
  ;; playground is the selected buffer — it usually is not when a reply lands.
  (ed-update-named m (buffer-name spec) f))

;; The request book (`[key :pending]`) is written and read only through `session`'s
;; `client-sent` / `client-unpend` / `client-reply`, whose `sig`s keep a slot named by the
;; spec's key reading as a model to the checker.

(defn live-buffer? (spec m)
  "Is this playground on screen — i.e. does it still want a session? One of the
`sandbox-events` client hooks; it is what stops a dead child being let go while the
buffer is open."
  (not (nil? (pg-index spec m))))

(defn- live? ()
  ;; Is there a running editor to deliver an event to (`model/ed-headless?`)? Without the
  ;; `ui-run` loop a fire-and-forget request has nowhere to come back to, so the launcher
  ;; falls back to the in-image path instead of stranding every form on `evaluating…`.
  (not (ed-headless?)))

;; ---- the forms, and which of them changed -----------------------------------------
;;
;; A language's `:forms-of` builds a positioned CST of the whole buffer, so only the LAUNCH
;; parses, and what it caches on the buffer is the currency everything else spends:
;;
;;   `:playground-spans`  index -> [start end], for finding which form a point is in
;;   `:playground-deps`   index -> what it defines / uses, for what a reply records
;;
;; Both describe `:playground-rope`, and are exact for exactly as long as the buffer still
;; holds it — an O(1) comparison, and the one both later readers make.

(defn form-srcs-in (text forms)
  "`{index -> source}` for already-parsed `forms` — the shape a launch compares against
last time's to find what changed."
  (fold (seq/enumerate forms)
    {}
    (fn (acc iv)
      (let ([i node] iv) (assoc acc i (string/substring text (:start node) (:end node)))))))

(defn form-spans-in (text forms)
  "`[[start end] …]` for already-parsed `forms`, in order — the extents a launch caches so
that finding which form the cursor is in later costs a scan rather than a parse."
  (into [] (map forms (fn (node) [(:start node) (:end node)]))))

(defn form-index-in (spans point)
  "Which form `point` falls in, given [[form-spans-in]] extents, or nil. The only place
that answers \"which form is this?\" — a scan over extents, never a parse."
  (first (seq/filter (range (count spans))
           (fn (i) (let ([lo hi] (nth spans i)) (and (>= point lo) (<= point hi)))))))

(defn evaluated-src (evaluated i)
  "The source the session has evaluated for form `i`, or nil when it has evaluated none —
because the form is new, because it changed, or because a reply for it never landed."
  (map/get-in evaluated [i :src]))

(defn index-set (indices)
  "`indices` as a set, to be asked with `contains?` — the membership test you can afford
to make in a loop.

The trap this exists to name: **`includes?` does not take a set's fast path.** It is the
SEQUENCE membership question (`index-of`), and it scans — 64× slower than `contains?` on
500 elements, which probes the CHAMP hash directly. Every hot loop here asks about a set
of form indices once per form, so reaching for the familiar `includes?` turns each of
them quadratic — on the keystroke path."
  (into #{} indices))

(defn pg-unevaluate (evaluated indices)
  "Forget what the session evaluated for every form in `indices` — what a launch does to
the forms it is SENDING, so that a reply which never lands leaves the form visibly
un-evaluated and the next launch sends it again."
  (let (dropping (index-set indices))
    (into {} (seq/filter evaluated (fn (kv) (not (contains? dropping (first kv))))))))

(defn pg-evaluated (spec m i src deps)
  "Record that the session has evaluated `src` for form `i`, with its `deps` — what a
landing reply (or the in-image path) leaves behind."
  (pg-update-buf spec
    m
    (fn (bb)
      (assoc bb
        :playground-evaluated (assoc (or (:playground-evaluated bb) {}) i {:src src :deps deps})))))

(defn realign-evaluated (srcs evaluated total)
  "Re-key what the session has evaluated to the forms as they are NOW: `{:aligned {index ->
record} :dropped (record …) :pairs ([old-index new-index] …)}`.

The records are keyed by index, and an index is not an identity: inserting a form (or
splitting one) shifts every form below it down a slot, and compared slot-for-slot every
one of them read as changed — adding a line near the top re-evaluated the whole buffer,
which is exactly the cost the launch plan exists to avoid. So a record follows its TEXT:
walking the current forms in order, each takes the first not-yet-taken record below the
last match whose source is identical. In-order, so a duplicated form (two `f(1)`s) pairs
with its own record and not its twin's. What matches nothing is `:dropped` — a form that
was edited or deleted — and its old `:defines` still matter to [[stale-forms]], since the
callers of a definition that vanished must be re-run to lose the answer it gave them.
Each dropped record carries `:before`: the current index of the first surviving form that
stood below it (`total` when none did), which is where it stood, so that what it defined
is provided from THERE — the downward-only model holds for a vanished definition as for a
live one."
  ;; Bucketed by source, ascending within each bucket, so pairing a form is a lookup in
  ;; the handful of records that hold its exact text rather than a scan of every record
  ;; there is — this runs on the KEYSTROKE path ([[ed-reanchor]] aligns the notes on every
  ;; edit), where a scan per form is quadratic.
  (let (olds (sort (keys evaluated))
        by-src (fold (reverse olds)
                 ; consing over descending keys leaves each bucket ascending
                 {}
                 (fn (acc j)
                   (let (src (evaluated-src evaluated j))
                     (assoc acc src (cons j (get acc src '()))))))
        walked (fold (range total)
                 [{} '() 0]
                 (fn (acc i)
                   (let ([aligned pairs cursor] acc
                         ;; the first not-yet-taken record holding this exact source
                         j (seq/find (get by-src (get srcs i) '()) (fn (j) (>= j cursor))))
                     (if (nil? j)
                       acc
                       [(assoc aligned i (get evaluated j)) (cons [j i] pairs) (inc j)]))))
        [aligned pairs _] walked
        ;; asked with `contains?`, never `includes?` — see [[index-set]]
        matched (into #{} (map pairs first))
        ascending (reverse pairs) ; `[old new]`, in old order
        before-of (fn (j) (or (second (seq/find ascending (fn (ji) (> (first ji) j)))) total))
        dropped (map (seq/filter olds (fn (j) (not (contains? matched j))))
                  (fn (j) (assoc (get evaluated j) :before (before-of j)))))
    {:aligned aligned :dropped dropped :pairs ascending}))

(defn changed-forms (srcs evaluated total)
  "The indices whose source is not what the session has evaluated — a form someone edited,
one that has just appeared, and one whose reply never came back. `()` when the session is
up to date. `evaluated` is compared slot-for-slot, so pass it through
[[realign-evaluated]] first unless the forms are known not to have moved."
  (seq/filter (range total) (fn (i) (not (= (get srcs i) (evaluated-src evaluated i))))))

(defn- reaches? (references defines)
  ;; Does any of `references` name one of `defines`? A qualified reference matches its own
  ;; bare name too: inside a Brood `(defmodule bar)` buffer a `(defn foo …)` defines `foo`
  ;; and is called as `bar/foo`; in Elixir a function defined in `defmodule Foo` is called
  ;; as `Foo.bar`. Matching the last segment over-matches (a local `foo` and an unrelated
  ;; `other/foo` from a required module), which re-runs a form that did not need it — the
  ;; safe direction.
  (any? references
    (fn (r)
      (any? defines
        (fn (d) (or (= r d) (string/ends-with? r (str "/" d)) (string/ends-with? r (str "." d))))))))

(defn- dropped-defines (dropped)
  ;; `{index -> names}`: what each of [[realign-evaluated]]'s `:dropped` records defined,
  ;; provided from where its form stood (its `:before`), in record order.
  (fold dropped
    {}
    (fn (acc record)
      (let (at (:before record))
        (assoc acc at (append (get acc at '()) (get (:deps record) :defines '())))))))

(defn- stale-step (spec srcs deps changed vanished)
  ;; [[stale-forms]]' fold step over the forms in order, `[provided stale tail?] -> …`:
  ;; `provided` is every name a re-run form above (or a vanished one, from where it stood)
  ;; defines. A form is stale when it changed, reaches one of those, or sits in the tail
  ;; below a form that changes what names mean.
  (fn (acc i)
    (let ([provided-above stale tail?] acc
          provided (append provided-above (get vanished i '()))
          form-deps (nth deps i {}))
      (if (or tail? (contains? changed i) (reaches? (get form-deps :references '()) provided))
        [(append provided (get form-deps :defines '()))
          (cons i stale)
          (or tail? (svc spec :namespace-form? (list (get srcs i ""))))]
        [provided stale tail?]))))

(defn stale-forms (spec srcs evaluated deps total)
  "Which forms a launch must re-send: every one whose own source changed, plus every LATER
form that reaches one of them — directly, or through another form that is being re-run.
`evaluated` is what [[realign-evaluated]] returns, so a form that only MOVED is not stale.

\"Everything from the first change down\" (the suffix rule) is correct and expensive:
editing the second of twenty forms would re-evaluate nineteen, on every settle of the
keyboard. Most edits are to a CALL, and nothing below a call can see it. So the question is
not \"is this form below the edit?\" but \"does this form use anything the edit could have
changed?\", which the language's `:deps-of` answers.

A re-run form provides what it defines NOW; what its previous text defined arrives through
[[realign-evaluated]]'s `:dropped` records, so renaming `foo` to `bar` still reaches the
forms that called `foo`, which now deserve an error instead of a stale answer.

Downward only: a form cannot depend on text below it. Two things this cannot see, and both
are handled by widening rather than by guessing — a form that changes what names mean below
it (the spec's `:namespace-form?`) takes the whole tail, and a `deps` that does not line up
with the forms one-for-one falls back to the suffix rule."
  (let ({:aligned aligned :dropped dropped} evaluated
        changed (index-set (changed-forms srcs aligned total))
        vanished (dropped-defines dropped)
        walked (fold (range total) ['() '() false] (stale-step spec srcs deps changed vanished)))
    (reverse (second walked))))

(defn launch-plan (spec srcs realigned deps total)
  "The indices a launch re-sends: [[stale-forms]] when the dependency data lines up with
the forms one-for-one, else every form from the first change down.

`realigned` is [[realign-evaluated]]'s answer for what the session knows — the same shape
[[stale-forms]] takes, and passed in rather than computed here because the caller re-keys
its own records with that same alignment: one pairing, so what the launch re-sends and
what it writes back as evaluated answer the same question.

The deps fallback is not defensive padding. A language whose `:deps-of` cannot answer at
all returns `()` and gets the suffix rule; and a language whose deps and forms ever
disagreed would be off by one and re-run the wrong forms, silently. Re-running too much is
the failure worth having."
  (if (= (count deps) total)
    (stale-forms spec srcs realigned deps total)
    (let (from (first (changed-forms srcs (:aligned realigned) total)))
      (if (nil? from) '() (range from total)))))

;; ---- notes: a result at the end of its form ----------------------------------------

(defn pg-placement (rope text spans)
  "Everything placing a note needs about the buffer as it stands: where each form is, the
text to slice a form's source out of, and the ROPE to ask which line an offset is on.

The rope is the load-bearing member — it indexes lines, so `eolnotes/answer-line` is
O(log n) per note. Asked of the TEXT the same question splits the whole buffer per note,
O(notes × buffer) on the KEYSTROKE path."
  {:rope rope :text text :spans spans})

;; A note's form is remembered as ONE MARKER at its END — `std/editor/buffer`'s
;; edit-surviving positions, the facility built for \"overlays, diagnostics, and remote
;; participants' cursors\". Every editing op shifts it with the text, so after a keystroke
;; the form is still `[end - length, end)` with no parsing to find it again. That is what
;; makes [[ed-reanchor]] O(notes) instead of O(buffer).
;;
;; **The start is derived, not marked, and that is the point.** Markers have LEFT gravity —
;; an insert exactly AT one leaves it put, so that text typed at your cursor lands after a
;; collaborator's sitting in the same spot. Right for a cursor, wrong for a form's opening
;; bracket: type at the very start of a form and its start marker would stay behind while
;; the form moved down, and the note would read its own form as changed. The end has the
;; gravity a form's end wants, and while the source is UNCHANGED — the only case where the
;; note still applies — its length gives the start exactly.
(defn- pg-note-marker (bb key hi) (buffer-marker-set bb [:pg-end key] hi))

(defn- pg-intact-region (bb rope key src)
  ;; `[lo hi]` for `key`'s form as the text stands now — when it still has its marker AND
  ;; still holds `src`, the source its note was stamped for — else nil.
  (let (hi (buffer-marker bb [:pg-end key]))
    (when hi
      (let (lo (- hi (count src)))
        (when (and (>= lo 0) (= (text/slice rope lo hi) src)) [lo hi])))))

(defn- pg-place-rows (note field first-line)
  ;; A note's `:hints` and `:inlays` arrive with `:offset` — the line WITHIN the form each
  ;; one describes, because the child only ever saw that form's text and counts from its
  ;; first line. The buffer line is the form's first line plus that offset, and this is the
  ;; one place that knows where the form sits, so it is the one place that converts.
  ;; Everything downstream deals in buffer lines like every other note.
  ;;
  ;; The two fields are separate because they are painted somewhere else entirely — an
  ;; inlay is a row above the line rather than the end of it — and a producer chooses per
  ;; entry which a piece of derived text deserves: a signature belongs above the definition
  ;; it describes, a `dbg` step's value belongs at the step.
  (let (rows (get note field))
    (if (empty? rows)
      note
      (assoc note field (map rows (fn (r) (assoc r :line (+ first-line (or (:offset r) 0)))))))))

(defn- pg-note-line (spec rope lo hi)
  ;; Where the answer for the form at `[lo hi)` sits: the language's `:note-line`, else the
  ;; line below the form's last (`eolnotes/answer-line`; the extent's end is exclusive).
  (if (has? spec :note-line)
    (svc spec :note-line (list rope lo hi))
    (eolnotes/answer-line rope (dec hi))))

(defn- pg-anchor-note (spec bb key note rope lo hi)
  ;; Paint `note` under `key` for the form at `[lo hi)` through the shared anchored-note
  ;; channel: its answer where [[pg-note-line]] says, its `:hint` at the form's opening line.
  ;; `eolnotes/set-note` owns everything else — the owner-keying, the hint key, and the
  ;; one-line collapse when a form at EOF has no line below it.
  (let (first-line (eolnotes/line-of rope lo))
    (eolnotes/set-note bb
      key
      (pg-place-rows (pg-place-rows note :hints first-line) :inlays first-line)
      (pg-note-line spec rope lo hi)
      first-line)))

(defn- pg-note-into (spec bb key note span place)
  ;; Stamp `note` under `key` on buffer `bb`, anchored to the form at `span`: what it says
  ;; and the source it says it of go to `:playground-notes`, the form's end gets its marker,
  ;; and the note is painted. `key` and `span` are passed apart because a note keeps the
  ;; index its launch gave it while its form may since have moved to another slot.
  (if (nil? span)
    bb
    (let ([lo hi] span
          {:rope rope :text text} place
          said {:src (string/substring text lo hi) :note (dissoc note :line)})
      (pg-anchor-note spec
        (pg-note-marker (map/assoc-in bb [:playground-notes key] said) key hi)
        key
        note
        rope
        lo
        hi))))

(defn pg-set-note (spec m key note place &optional (at nil))
  "[[pg-note-into]] on this playground's buffer: `note` under `key`, anchored to the form
at index `at` — which defaults to `key`, because the two differ only when a form has moved
out from under a key already spoken for (see the reply path)."
  (let (i (or at key))
    (pg-update-buf spec
      m
      (fn (bb) (pg-note-into spec bb key note (nth (:spans place) i nil) place)))))

(defn- pg-form-pairs (b srcs total)
  ;; `([stash-key current-index] …)`: each stashed note matched to the form it now
  ;; describes, by TEXT — [[realign-evaluated]]'s in-order pairing over the sources those
  ;; notes were stamped for. Keys are the indices their stamping launch used; this says
  ;; which form each one now belongs to, and a note whose form has since changed pairs with
  ;; nothing.
  (:pairs (realign-evaluated srcs (or (:playground-notes b) {}) total)))

(defn- pg-rekey-notes (stash pairs)
  ;; The stashed notes re-keyed from their stamping launch's indices to the current ones
  ;; through `pairs`, unwrapped to bare notes; one whose form matches nothing is dropped,
  ;; since that form changed and the launch re-derives it.
  (fold pairs
    {}
    (fn (acc ji) (let ([j i] ji e (get stash j)) (if e (assoc acc i (:note e)) acc)))))

(defn- pg-clear-anchors (bb)
  ;; Drop the ANCHORED notes, keeping what they say (`:playground-notes`). What a re-anchor
  ;; does before re-placing them: a note whose form is not on screen right now must not
  ;; paint at the line that form used to be on, but it is still the right note for it.
  (annotate bb :eol-notes {}))

(defn- pg-clear-notes (bb)
  ;; Drop every note, anchor and text alike. A launch rebuilds the map from nothing, so a
  ;; note for a form that has been deleted cannot survive — the alternative (patching in
  ;; place) leaves the result of a form that no longer exists sitting on whatever line took
  ;; its place.
  (dissoc (pg-clear-anchors bb) :playground-notes))

;; ---- eval-on-type: the stale? / launch / apply triple --------------------------------

(defn ed-stale? (spec m)
  "O(1): the selected buffer is this playground and its rope moved since the last launch —
or a fresh session asked for a replay (`:playground-replay`) — which arms the idle timer."
  (let (b (ed-current-buf m))
    (and b
      (= (:name b) (buffer-name spec))
      (or (:playground-replay b) (not (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b)))))))

(defn- pg-reanchor-marked (spec bb rope stash)
  ;; The fast path: one marker read and one slice per note, no parsing. A note whose form
  ;; still holds the source it was stamped for is re-placed at the line it now sits on; one
  ;; whose form changed is un-placed but not forgotten, so reverting the edit puts it back.
  (fold stash
    (pg-clear-anchors bb)
    (fn (acc kv)
      (let ([key entry] kv
            region (pg-intact-region acc rope key (:src entry)))
        (if (nil? region)
          acc
          (let ([lo hi] region) (pg-anchor-note spec acc key (:note entry) rope lo hi)))))))

(defn- pg-reanchor-by-text (spec bb rope stash)
  ;; The slow path, for when a note has lost its marker: find each form by TEXT instead.
  ;; Undo restores the markers its snapshot recorded, and a snapshot taken before a note
  ;; was stamped has none — so undoing PAST a landed reply takes that note's marker with
  ;; it. The note is still right and its form is still there; only the shortcut to it is
  ;; gone. This costs one parse, and running it unconditionally is what the marker avoids.
  (let (text (text/->string rope)
        forms (svc spec :forms-of (list text))
        spans (form-spans-in text forms)
        pairs (:pairs (realign-evaluated (form-srcs-in text forms) stash (count forms))))
    (fold pairs
      (pg-clear-anchors bb)
      (fn (acc ji)
        (let ([key i] ji
              entry (get stash key)
              span (nth spans i nil))
          (if (or (nil? entry) (nil? span))
            acc
            (let ([lo hi] span)
              ;; re-mark it, so the next edit is fast again
              (pg-anchor-note spec (pg-note-marker acc key hi) key (:note entry) rope lo hi))))))))

(defn ed-reanchor (spec m)
  "Re-anchor this playground's notes to the text as it is NOW — on the edit itself, not the
debounce. A note is stored with the line it sits on, and `eolnotes/eol-notes` hides the whole
set the moment the line count changes, since a stored line may no longer be the form's —
without this, every result would vanish on Enter until the launch re-stamped them.

**Each note's form is a MARKER**, which the toolkit shifts with the text on every edit. So
this reads one marker and one slice per note: unchanged source, and the note is re-placed at
the line it now sits on; changed, and it is un-placed but not forgotten
(`:playground-notes` keeps what it says), so reverting the edit puts it back. Evaluation
stays on the debounce — this moves lines and nothing else.

**The guard is the annotation's own currency, not a remembered rope.** A REPLY lands
whenever the session answers and stamps against the text of that moment, so a remembered
launch rope says nothing about whether the notes fit the text now (type a newline, get a
result, delete the newline: the buffer is back at the launch's rope with its notes anchored
to a line count that no longer exists). Asking whether the notes are stamped against THIS
rope is the same O(1) test and cannot drift from what it is guarding.

The keystroke is also what opens the quiet window, provisionally (see the quiet window)."
  (let (m (pg-quiet-on-edit spec m)
        b (pg-buf spec m))
    (cond
      (nil? b) m
      (not (eolnotes/eol-notes-stale? b)) m
      else
        (let (rope (buffer-rope b)
              stash (or (:playground-notes b) {})
              ;; every note still carries its marker? then no parsing is needed at all
              marked? (every? (keys stash) (fn (k) (not (nil? (buffer-marker b [:pg-end k]))))))
          (pg-update-buf spec
            m
            (fn (bb)
              (if marked?
                (pg-reanchor-marked spec bb rope stash)
                (pg-reanchor-by-text spec bb rope stash))))))))

(defn- pending-of (spec m) (map/get-in m [(get spec :key) :pending] {}))

;; ---- held forms: ready, but not run until asked (issues.md S5) -----------------------------
;; A playground runs every form as you stop typing — and an electric pair closes
;; `(file/spit "notes.txt" "")` a moment before the text goes in. So a form whose running
;; reaches outside the session (the language's `:held?`) waits, noted, until C-c C-c. The
;; release is keyed by the form's SOURCE: edit the form and it is a new form, held again.

(defn- pg-held-reason (spec b src)
  "Why form `src` is held — the language's `:held?` answer — or nil when it runs as usual:
the language holds nothing, or this exact text was released."
  (when (and (has? spec :held?) (not (contains? (or (:playground-released b) #{}) src)))
    (svc spec :held? (list src))))

(defn- pg-release (bb i src)
  ;; Release form `i`, whose source is `src`: not evaluated, so the launch sends it; replay,
  ;; so a launch happens although the text has not moved.
  (assoc bb
    :playground-released (conj (or (:playground-released bb) #{}) src)
    :playground-evaluated (pg-unevaluate (or (:playground-evaluated bb) {}) (list i))
    :playground-replay true))

(defn pg-release-at-point (spec m)
  "C-c C-c in a playground: run the held form at point, once — the next launch sends it.
Says so when point is not on a form, or the buffer has moved on since the last launch
parsed it (the form's extent is not known yet; a moment later it is)."
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m)
        spans (when b (or (:playground-spans b) []))
        i (when b (form-index-in spans (buffer-point b))))
    (cond
      (nil? b) m
      (not (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b)))
        (message m "still reading the buffer — try again")
      (nil? i) (message m "no form at point")
      else
        (let ([lo hi] (nth spans i)
              src (text/slice (buffer-rope b) lo hi))
          (message (pg-update-buf spec m (fn (bb) (pg-release bb i src))) "running the held form")))))

(defn- pg-known (spec m b)
  ;; What the session knows about each form: what it has actually EVALUATED, plus the forms
  ;; whose identical text is already IN FLIGHT — those need no second request, their answer
  ;; is coming. A request carries the `:deps` it was sent with, so the two kinds are the
  ;; same shape and this map is homogeneous: a form displaced by an in-flight record still
  ;; contributes what it defines to `stale-forms`, which a record without deps would
  ;; silently have dropped.
  (merge (or (:playground-evaluated b) {}) (liveeval/in-flight (pending-of spec m))))

(defn- pg-launch-reset (spec m pairs stale launched)
  ;; Before anything runs: move the pane's cached reports with their forms (`pairs`) and
  ;; drop the cascades of everything being re-sent — they describe code that no longer
  ;; exists; a form nobody re-ran keeps a report that is still true — then stamp the
  ;; buffer with what this launch parsed (`launched`) and clear its notes.
  (let (spy (get spec :spy))
    (pg-update-buf spec
      (workings/forget spy (workings/rekey spy m pairs) (fn (i) (contains? stale i)))
      (fn (bb) (pg-clear-notes (merge (dissoc bb :playground-replay) launched))))))

(defn- pg-in-image (spec m i src deps data note-at)
  ;; Headless, there is no loop to deliver a reply to: the language's `:in-image` answer,
  ;; noted, recorded as evaluated and handed to `:on-verdict` — or nil. A language whose
  ;; session IS a child process has no in-image answer to give, so headless it answers
  ;; nothing.
  (let (reply (when (has? spec :in-image) (svc spec :in-image (list src))))
    (when reply
      (let (note (svc-form spec :note-of (list reply src) data i))
        (pg-hook spec
          :on-verdict (pg-evaluated spec (note-at m i note) i src deps)
          i
          note
          (nth data i nil))))))

(defn- pg-launch-client (spec b deps data note-at)
  ;; This playground's half of `liveeval/form-step`: how it sends, what a request records
  ;; (its `:deps`, which `in-flight` hands back to the plan), and the in-image path.
  {:note-at note-at
   ;; ready, but it reaches outside the session and nobody asked — C-c C-c releases it
   ;; ([[pg-release-at-point]])
   :held-reason (fn (src) (pg-held-reason spec b src))
   :held-how (get spec :held-how)
   :live? (live?)
   :send (fn (id i src) (svc-form spec :eval (list id src (get spec :timeout-ms)) data i))
   :request (fn (i src) {:region i :src src :deps (nth deps i {})})
   :no-session (str "no session — " (get spec :restart))
   :headless (fn (m i src) (pg-in-image spec m i src (nth deps i {}) data note-at))})

(defn- pg-answered (spec acc i note data note-at)
  ;; A form the language graded itself (`[:answered note]` from `:parse-state`): its note,
  ;; and an answer like any other for `:on-verdict`. Nil says nothing at all.
  (let ([m next-id pending] acc)
    (if (nil? note)
      acc
      [(pg-hook spec :on-verdict (note-at m i note) i note (nth data i nil)) next-id pending])))

(defn- pg-settled-here? (state held?)
  ;; Did this launch settle the form without asking the session? Broken, mid-typing, blank,
  ;; graded locally, or held — none of them is waiting on anything. A ready form is not: it
  ;; was sent, answered in-image (recorded there), or could not be sent and should be tried
  ;; again.
  (or (not (= state :ok)) held?))

(defn- pg-form-step (spec b srcs deps data stale old-notes place)
  ;; The launch's fold step, `(acc i) -> acc`: each form takes its turn through the step
  ;; every live client shares (`liveeval/form-step`) — carry, note, hold, send or answer
  ;; in-image. A form the plan did not reach is carried with its last note.
  ;;
  ;; A form re-derived but SETTLED here ([[pg-settled-here?]]) is recorded as evaluated
  ;; with the text it was settled for. Not because the session holds it — because nothing
  ;; is coming for it, and a form left un-evaluated is "changed" at every launch: for a
  ;; language without deps (the suffix rule) one broken form near the top re-sent the whole
  ;; tail below it on every settle of the keyboard.
  (let (note-at (fn (m i note) (pg-set-note spec m i note place))
        client (pg-launch-client spec b deps data note-at))
    (fn (acc i)
      (let (src (get srcs i "")
            state (if (contains? stale i) (svc-form spec :parse-state (list src) data i) :carry)
            next (match state
                   ([:answered note] (pg-answered spec acc i note data note-at))
                   (_ (liveeval/form-step acc i src state (get old-notes i) client))))
        (if (and (not (= state :carry))
              (pg-settled-here? state (and (= state :ok) (pg-held-reason spec b src))))
          (let ([m next-id pending] next)
            [(pg-evaluated spec m i src (nth deps i {})) next-id pending])
          next)))))

;; ---- the quiet window: a new error waits until you stop typing ---------------------
;; Every pause evaluates, and an electric pair makes every prefix of a form a complete one:
;; `(defn` is `(defn )` and RUNS — `fn: missing parameter list` — and `(dbl ` is `(dbl )`,
;; `expected 1 argument`. Each is true and none is news; it is what typing looks like. So
;; the form at point, once you have changed it, is in a QUIET WINDOW: a new error for it
;; is held (`liveeval/typing-note`, the pending `…`) and the checker's findings on its lines
;; are kept out of the margin (`diagstore/ed-without-quiet-lines`). Results that SUCCEED land at
;; once — that is the point of evaluating as you type. The window closes when you pause
;; (`:quiet-ms`, [[quiet-timer]]) or move off the form ([[on-move]]), and everything it held
;; lands then, exactly as it would have.
;;
;; An error the form was already showing is not held: you are looking at it, and holding
;; its update would make a form you are FIXING look fixed for a second.
;;
;; The window is the buffer's `:playground-quiet` — `{:region :src :lines :erring? :held}` —
;; and `:quiet-lines`, the generic `[from to]` the diagnostics index reads. Opt-in by the
;; spec's `:quiet-ms`: the tutorial grades what you type and has no such window.

(def *quiet-ms*
  ;; How long after your last key a held error waits — long enough to outlast the gap
  ;; between words, short enough that a real mistake is on screen by the time you look.
  1200)

(defn- pg-note-within (b lo hi)
  ;; The note the form now at `[lo hi]` was showing before this edit, or nil — found by its
  ;; end marker, which every edit inside the form has carried along with the text.
  (let (hit (seq/find (or (:playground-notes b) {})
              (fn (kv)
                (let (end (buffer-marker b [:pg-end (first kv)])) (and end (>= end lo) (<= end hi))))))
    (when hit (:note (second hit)))))

(defn- pg-erring? (b lo hi old i)
  ;; Was the form at `[lo hi]` (index `i`) showing an ERROR before this edit? Its note says
  ;; so, unless that note is still PENDING (`evaluating…`, or a held `…`) — a fast run of
  ;; edits replaces an error with `evaluating…` before the next answer is back — and then
  ;; the window this one replaces (`old`) remembers. A success in between clears it: a form
  ;; you have just FIXED is not one you are looking at an error on.
  (let (note (pg-note-within b lo hi))
    (if (and note (not (eolnotes/note-pending? (:kind note))))
      (= true (:error? note))
      (= true (and old (= (:region old) i) (:erring? old))))))

(defn- pg-quiet-window (spec m b srcs spans evaluated rope)
  ;; The window this launch opens, or nil: the form at point, when this playground keeps
  ;; one (`:quiet-ms`), is the buffer you are in, and the form's text is not what the
  ;; session last evaluated — you changed it. A replay (a fresh session) changed nothing.
  (let (i (form-index-in spans (:point b 0)))
    (when (and (has? spec :quiet-ms)
            (not (:playground-replay b))
            (= (:name (ed-current-buf m)) (buffer-name spec))
            i
            (not (= (get srcs i) (evaluated-src evaluated i))))
      (let ([lo hi] (nth spans i)
            old (:playground-quiet b))
        {:region i
         :src (get srcs i)
         :lines [(eolnotes/line-of rope lo) (eolnotes/line-of rope hi)]
         :erring? (pg-erring? b lo hi old i)}))))

(defn- pg-reindex-diagnostics (spec m)
  ;; The checker's findings re-indexed against the window as it now stands
  ;; (`diagstore/ed-without-quiet-lines` reads it) — from the list the model kept, so nothing is
  ;; re-checked. Only while this playground is the buffer those findings are about.
  (if (and (= (:name (ed-current-buf m)) (buffer-name spec)) (:diag-rope m))
    (diagstore/ed-set-diagnostics m (or (:diagnostics m) '()) (:diag-rope m))
    m))

(defn- pg-set-quiet (spec m quiet)
  ;; Open (or move) the window: `quiet` on the buffer, its lines where the diagnostics
  ;; index looks, and the index redone when those lines moved — a finding that landed a
  ;; moment before the window opened is about the typing all the same.
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m)
        moved? (not (= (:lines quiet) (:quiet-lines b)))
        m1 (pg-update-buf spec
             m
             (fn (bb) (assoc bb :playground-quiet quiet :quiet-lines (:lines quiet)))))
    (if moved? (pg-reindex-diagnostics spec m1) m1)))

(defn- pg-quiet-on-edit (spec m)
  ;; The KEYSTROKE opens the window, provisionally, on the line being typed: the checker's
  ;; idle beat (120ms) lands before the launch (250ms) that knows the form, and its findings
  ;; on `(dbl ` would otherwise flash up for the gap. No `:region` yet, so nothing is held
  ;; on it; the launch replaces it with the form's own window.
  (let (b (ed-current-buf m))
    (if (and (has? spec :quiet-ms)
          b
          (= (:name b) (buffer-name spec))
          (not (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b))))
      (let (line (buffer-current-line b)
            lines (:quiet-lines b))
        (if (and lines (>= line (first lines)) (<= line (second lines)))
          m
          (pg-set-quiet spec m (assoc (or (:playground-quiet b) {}) :lines [line line]))))
      m)))

(defn- pg-reveal-if-left (spec m)
  ;; A launch is a move too: when point is no longer in the form whose error the window
  ;; holds, that error lands FIRST — before the launch reads the notes it carries forward,
  ;; or the form (unchanged, so never re-sent) would carry its `…` on for good. Found by the
  ;; held form's own marker and text, with no parse; a form whose text has gone is past
  ;; holding, and [[reveal-quiet]] drops its answer.
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m))
    (if (or (nil? b) (nil? (map/get-in b [:playground-quiet :held :request])))
      m
      (let (request (map/get-in b [:playground-quiet :held :request])
            span (pg-intact-region b (buffer-rope b) (:region request) (:src request))
            point (buffer-point b))
        (if (and span (>= point (first span)) (<= point (second span)))
          m
          (reveal-quiet spec m))))))

(defn reveal-quiet (spec m)
  "Close this playground's quiet window: the error it held lands — note, pane, the
language's hooks — through the same path any reply takes, provided its form still holds the
text that was sent (it is silently dropped otherwise: you typed past it, and the launch for
what you typed has its own answer). The checker's findings on the window's lines come back
too, re-indexed from the list the model kept. `m` untouched when there is no window."
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m))
    (if (or (nil? b) (nil? (:playground-quiet b)))
      m
      (let (m1 (pg-update-buf spec m (fn (bb) (dissoc bb :playground-quiet :quiet-lines)))
            held (:held (:playground-quiet b)))
        (pg-reindex-diagnostics spec
          (if (and held (second (pg-answering spec b (:request held))))
            (pg-land-reply spec m1 (:request held) (:reply held))
            m1))))))

(defn quiet-timer (spec m)
  "The quiet window's timer, for `model/register-timer-client`: an idle one-shot `:quiet-ms`
after the last key while a window is open, nil otherwise. Firing it is [[reveal-quiet]]."
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m))
    (when (and b (:playground-quiet b)) {:after (get spec :quiet-ms) :idle? true})))

(defn ed-launch (spec m)
  "The debounce landed: re-evaluate the forms this edit could have changed the answer of —
the ones whose own text moved, plus the ones that use what those define ([[launch-plan]]).

A form that is mid-typing says so in the pending voice and keeps nothing stale; one that is
genuinely broken fails locally with no round trip; a blank one says nothing.

Notes are rebuilt, not patched — see the module docstring. A form whose reply is still in
flight wears `⋯ evaluating…` rather than nothing, so a rebuild never flashes the buffer
blank on its way to the same answer. A form nothing could reach (all of them, when the edit
was whitespace the parser ignores or an edit undone) is re-stamped with its last note where
it now sits — a form that moved takes its note with it."
  (let (m (pg-reveal-if-left spec m)
        b (pg-buf spec m))
    (if (nil? b)
      m
      (let (text (buffer-text b)
            rope (buffer-rope b)
            forms (pg-text-svc spec :forms-of m b text)
            total (count forms)
            srcs (form-srcs-in text forms)
            spans (form-spans-in text forms)
            deps (pg-text-svc spec :deps-of m b text)
            ;; ONE alignment, for the plan and for the write-back both — a form that moved
            ;; keeps its record. What goes back to `:playground-evaluated` is this minus the
            ;; promises, because only a reply landing may say the session holds a form
            ;; (see the module docstring).
            realigned (realign-evaluated srcs (pg-known spec m b) total)
            stale (index-set (launch-plan spec srcs realigned deps total))
            ;; each form of the last launch matched to where it is now — the notes AND the
            ;; pane's cached reports are keyed by the old indices, and both move with it
            pairs (pg-form-pairs b srcs total)
            old-notes (pg-rekey-notes (or (:playground-notes b) {}) pairs)
            ;; A form being SENT is no longer one the session has evaluated — only its
            ;; reply landing puts it back. A launch that recorded what it asked for would
            ;; leave a lost reply invisible forever.
            evaluated (pg-unevaluate (liveeval/settled (:aligned realigned)) stale)
            quiet (pg-quiet-window spec m b srcs spans (:aligned realigned) rope)
            reset (pg-launch-reset spec
                    m
                    pairs
                    stale
                    {:playground-rope rope
                     :playground-spans spans
                     :playground-deps deps
                     :playground-evaluated evaluated})
            m0 (if quiet (pg-set-quiet spec reset quiet) reset)
            step (pg-form-step spec
                   b
                   srcs
                   deps
                   (form-data spec m)
                   stale
                   old-notes
                   (pg-placement rope text spans))
            [m1 next-id sent] (fold (range total) [m0 (session/next-id m) {}] step))
        (pg-hook spec
          :after-launch (session/client-sent m1 (get spec :key) next-id (merge (pending-of spec m1) sent)))))))

;; ---- registering a client: every table a live buffer runs through, in one call ---------

(defn register-client (client)
  "Register a live-evaluating buffer with every table its life runs through — called once,
when the client's module loads. `client` names its hooks as symbols (resolved late, so a
redefinition hot-swaps):

- `:timer` `:stale?` `:launch` `:reanchor` — eval-on-type, on the model's idle debounce
  (`model/register-live-eval-client`)
- `:quiet-timer` `:quiet-spec` `:quiet-fire` — the quiet window's timer
  (`model/register-timer-client`); left out by a client with no quiet window
- `:session` `:live?` `:ready` `:reply` `:down` — its evaluation session's events
  (`sandbox-events/register-client`), which is also what loads `sandbox-events`, so its
  impls are in place before the buffer can have started a session"
  (do
    (model/register-live-eval-client (map/select-keys client [:timer :stale? :launch :reanchor]))
    (when (get client :quiet-timer)
      (model/register-timer-client (get client :quiet-timer)
        (get client :quiet-spec)
        (get client :quiet-fire)))
    (sandbox-events/register-client (map/select-keys client [:session :live? :ready :reply :down]))))

;; ---- the session client hooks ---------------------------------------------------------

(defn sandbox-ready (spec m gen)
  "The child announced itself (first boot, or a reset): remember its generation and clear
the eval stash so the next idle beat replays the whole buffer against the fresh, empty
session. Clearing `:playground-evaluated` is the load-bearing half — leaving it behind
would make the restart re-send nothing, and every form would look evaluated against a
session that no longer holds any of its definitions.

`:pending` is KEPT. A request sent before the banner queued in the worker, and the worker
flushes its queue to the child that just readied — so those replies are coming, carrying
this generation, and are the answers to the forms as they stand (dropped, they would land
on no pending entry and the form would be re-sent on every settled keystroke). A request
that was IN FLIGHT when a reset killed the child was answered
`{:timeout true}` by the worker before the respawn, so nothing is left dangling."
  (pg-update-buf spec
    (session/client-ready m (get spec :key) gen)
    (fn (bb)
      ;; `:playground-replay` is what makes the next idle beat launch: the buffer has not
      ;; moved, so [[ed-stale?]]'s rope comparison alone would not. The rope and extents of
      ;; the last launch STAY — they are what a note's key means against the text.
      (assoc bb :playground-replay true :playground-evaluated {}))))

(defn- pg-reply-view (spec b)
  ;; `{:text :spans :deps}` of the buffer as a reply finds it. The extents and deps cached
  ;; by the last launch are exact while the buffer still holds the text it parsed, which is
  ;; the ordinary case and costs no parse. When it does not — an edit landed while the
  ;; request was in flight — the answer may still be good, so both are re-derived rather
  ;; than thrown away.
  (let (text (buffer-text b))
    (if (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b))
      {:text text
       :spans (or (:playground-spans b) [])
       :deps (or (:playground-deps b) (svc spec :deps-of (list text)))}
      {:text text
       :spans (form-spans-in text (svc spec :forms-of (list text)))
       :deps (svc spec :deps-of (list text))})))

(defn answering-form (src-at total i0 sent)
  "Which of `total` forms a reply answers: the one whose source (`(src-at k)`) is still
`sent` — at the index `i0` it was sent from, or, when a line typed above it moved it while
the request was in flight, the NEAREST slot holding that text (nearest, so twin forms take
their own reply and not each other's). Nil when no form holds it any more."
  (if (= (src-at i0) sent)
    i0
    (seq/min-by (seq/filter (range total) (fn (k) (= (src-at k) sent)))
      (fn (k) (math/abs (- k i0))))))

(defn- pg-answering (spec b request)
  ;; `[view i]`: the buffer as a reply finds it ([[pg-reply-view]]) and the form that still
  ;; holds what `request` sent ([[answering-form]]), nil when none does.
  (let (view (pg-reply-view spec b)
        {:text text :spans spans} view
        src-at (fn (k)
                 (let (span (nth spans k nil))
                   (when span (string/substring text (first span) (second span))))))
    [view (answering-form src-at (count spans) (:region request) (:src request))]))

(defn- pg-hold? (b request note reply)
  ;; Is this answer a NEW error for the form being typed — one the quiet window holds? The
  ;; request must be the window's own send (its form and text), the note an error that is
  ;; not a timeout (that is the session, not your typing), and the form must not have been
  ;; showing an error already: an error you were already looking at is not news, and
  ;; hiding its update would make a form you are FIXING look fixed.
  (let (q (:playground-quiet b))
    (and q
      (= (:region request) (:region q))
      (= (:src request) (:src q))
      (:error? note)
      (not (get reply :timeout))
      (not (:erring? q)))))

(defn- pg-land-reply (spec m request reply)
  ;; Accepted when THE FORM's source is still the one that was sent — not the whole
  ;; buffer's rope: a form's answer does not go stale because a different form moved.
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m))
    (if (nil? b)
      m
      (let ([view i] (pg-answering spec b request)
            {:text text :spans spans :deps deps} view
            i0 (:region request)
            src (when i (let ([lo hi] (nth spans i)) (string/substring text lo hi)))
            data (form-data spec m)
            note (when i (svc-form spec :note-of (list reply src) data i)))
        (cond
          (nil? i)
            (do
              (log/warn (str (get spec :buffer)
                          ": reply "
                          (get reply :id)
                          " dropped — form "
                          i0
                          " no longer holds the text that was sent; it re-evaluates on the next idle beat"))
              m)
          ;; A new error while you are typing the form: recorded as evaluated (the session
          ;; did run it), painted as the pending `…`, and the reply kept whole for
          ;; [[reveal-quiet]] to land through this very function — note, pane and hooks —
          ;; once you stop.
          (pg-hold? b request note reply)
            (pg-update-buf spec
              (pg-evaluated spec
                (pg-set-note spec
                  m
                  i0
                  (liveeval/typing-note)
                  (pg-placement (buffer-rope b) text spans)
                  i)
                i
                src
                (nth deps i {}))
              (fn (bb) (map/assoc-in bb [:playground-quiet :held] {:request request :reply reply})))
          ;; Stamped under the key its own REQUEST was made with (`i0`) and anchored at the
          ;; slot the form now occupies (`i`). Under `i` alone it would land on whatever key
          ;; the launch gave a different form — because `i0` and `i` differ exactly when the
          ;; forms have shifted, so the slot this one moved into is somebody else's — and
          ;; quietly replace that form's note.
          ;;
          ;; `workings/show` caches this form's cascade and opens the pane only if the
          ;; cascade is real AND belongs to the form the cursor is in — so a background
          ;; re-eval never yanks the pane off what you are reading.
          ;;
          ;; Then the language's own two hooks: `:on-verdict` for this answer, `:after-reply`
          ;; for whatever it derives from the buffer as a whole.
          else
            (let (landed (workings/show (get spec :spy)
                           (pg-evaluated spec
                             (pg-set-note spec
                               m
                               i0
                               note
                               (pg-placement (buffer-rope b) text spans)
                               i)
                             i
                             src
                             (nth deps i {}))
                           i
                           (form-index-in spans (:point b 0))
                           reply
                           src))
              (pg-hook spec :after-reply (pg-hook spec :on-verdict landed i note (nth data i nil)))))))))

(defn sandbox-reply (spec m reply gen)
  "Fold one reply into the model. A reply this playground never sent, one from a superseded
child (`session/client-reply`, the rule every session client shares), or one whose form has been
edited since the send is dropped — the next idle beat re-evaluates. Otherwise the result
lands as the form's note and its trace as the pane's cascade."
  (session/client-reply m
    (get spec :key)
    reply
    gen
    (get spec :buffer)
    (fn (m1 request answer) (pg-land-reply spec m1 request answer))))

(defn sandbox-down (spec m code)
  "The child went away and somebody still wants a session ([[session/client-down]])."
  (session/client-down m
    (get spec :key)
    (fn (mm) (live-buffer? spec mm))
    code
    (merge {:gave-up (str (get spec :buffer) " session keeps dying — *Messages* may say why")
            :spawn-failed (get spec :spawn-failed-message "could not start the session")
            :restarting (str (get spec :buffer) " session restarting…")}
      (get spec :down-says {}))))

;; ---- the pane follows the cursor --------------------------------------------------------

(defn spy-on-screen? (spec m)
  "Is this playground's pane currently on screen? (`aside/open?` of its spy spec.)"
  (aside/open? (get spec :spy) m))

(defn doc-at-point (b)
  "What the cursor in buffer `b` is pointing at, as pane lines — the mode's `:doc-at`
service: the symbol under point, else the head of the call it is inside, with that name's
arglist, signature and docstring. `()` when neither names anything bound.

Read through the MODE rather than called directly, like every other language service here,
so the pane documents whatever language the buffer is in."
  (let (f (ed-mode-service b :doc-at))
    (or (when f
          (try
            (f (buffer-text b) (:point b 0))
            (catch e
              (do (log/warn (str "playground: doc-at service failed: " (error-message e))) nil))))
      '())))

(defn- pg-left-quiet (spec m b)
  ;; Moving off the form a quiet window is open on closes it ([[reveal-quiet]]). Asked only
  ;; while the extents are exact — the rope is the one the last launch parsed — because a
  ;; moving rope is you TYPING, and typing is what the window waits out; the launch after
  ;; it re-opens the window on wherever you are.
  (let (q (:playground-quiet b))
    (if (and q
          (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b))
          (not (= (form-index-in (or (:playground-spans b) []) (:point b 0)) (:region q))))
      (reveal-quiet spec m)
      m)))

(defn on-move (spec m)
  "The `:on-move` hook, whenever point or the text moved: keep the pane following the cursor
from form to form and showing the docs of whatever it is on, with no re-evaluation.

**Nothing here parses the buffer.** The extents cached by the last launch say which form the
cursor is in with a scan — and they are EXACT precisely when the rope is still the text that
launch parsed, which is an O(1) comparison the debounce already makes.

While the rope has moved on, the extents describe text that no longer exists and the pane
holds the form it was on. That is not an approximation to apologise for: typing inside a form
does not change which form you are in, and the debounce re-parses ~150ms later and corrects
the boundary case for free. The DOCS still track the cursor throughout — a token scan and an
image lookup — which is the half you want while typing a call."
  (let (b0 (ed-current-buf m))
    (if (not (and b0 (= (:name b0) (buffer-name spec))))
      m
      (let (m (pg-left-quiet spec m b0)
            b (ed-current-buf m)
            spy (get spec :spy)
            docs (doc-at-point b)
            moved? (not (= docs (workings/at-point-lines spy m)))
            m1 (if moved? (workings/at-point spy m docs) m)
            ;; the PANE's key, not this playground's: the region the pane is showing is
            ;; the pane's state, written by `workings/refresh` under its own path
            here (map/get-in m1 [(get spy :key) :workings-region])
            i (when (= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b))
                (form-index-in (or (:playground-spans b) []) (:point b 0))))
        (cond
          ;; the form changed (or is showing for the first time): `follow` decides whether
          ;; that is worth opening a closed pane for
          (and i (not (= i here))) (workings/follow spy m1 i)
          ;; same form, new docs: re-render what is already open, never open one for a hover
          (and moved? (not (nil? (or i here)))) (workings/refresh spy m1 (or i here) false)
          else m1)))))

;; ---- the pane's colouring -----------------------------------------------------------
;; A spec's `:spy-faces` is the pane's colouring as DATA — `[test text role]` rows, the
;; first matching row wins — because the clients' panes differ by a heading and an arrow.
;; Roles rather than faces: a face built when the spec is `def`'d would keep the colours of
;; the theme that was current then.

(defn- spy-role-face (role)
  ;; Read at paint time, so a theme switch recolours the pane.
  (case role
    :dim {:fg *subtext*}
    :aside {:fg *overlay0* :italic true}
    :value {:fg *teal*}
    nil))

(defn- spy-rule-matches? (rule line)
  (let ([test text _] rule)
    (case test
      :prefix (string/starts-with? line text)
      :trimmed-prefix (string/starts-with? (string/trim-left line) text)
      :includes (includes? line text)
      false)))

(defn spy-fontify (spec src)
  "The pane's colouring from the spec's `:spy-faces`: the heading dim, every returned value
in the accent — so a cascade reads as calls going in and values coming back rather than as
a wall of text."
  (let (rules (get spec :spy-faces []))
    (modes/fontify-line-faces src
      (fn (line)
        (let (rule (seq/find rules (fn (r) (spy-rule-matches? r line))))
          (when rule (spy-role-face (nth rule 2))))))))

;; ---- opening and restarting ---------------------------------------------------------
;; The two commands every playground has, written once. What differs per language is
;; which session to start and what to say, and both are on the spec.

(defn- open-message (spec m booting?)
  ;; `:open-message` is a sentence, or a symbol `(m booting?) -> sentence` for a language
  ;; whose session takes long enough to boot that the wait needs explaining.
  (let (said (get spec :open-message))
    (if (string? said) said (svc spec :open-message (list m booting?)))))

(defn- pg-reselect (spec m)
  ;; The playground was already open and is now selected: bring the pane back onto the form
  ;; at point. The buffer may have moved on since the last launch, so this parses rather
  ;; than trusting the cached extents.
  (let (b (pg-buf spec m))
    (if (nil? b)
      m
      (let (text (buffer-text b)
            spans (form-spans-in text (pg-text-svc spec :forms-of m b text)))
        (workings/reopen (get spec :spy) m (or (form-index-in spans (:point b 0)) 0))))))

(defn open (spec m)
  "Open this playground, or switch to it: the session starts (only a running editor can
receive its replies, so a headless open starts nothing), and the buffer is created with the
pane beside it — or, already open, selected, with the pane brought back onto the form at
point.

**One command, not two.** Asking again switches to the buffer you already have and reopens
a pane you closed — the same intent (\"show me the playground\"). Closing the pane still
latches, so a later result does not reopen it behind you; asking for the playground again
does, because that is you asking.

The pane opens with `force` on a fresh buffer, rather than waiting to earn its split from
the first traced reply the way the tutorial's does: a lesson is prose you are reading and
the pane an aside, but a playground IS the pane's subject — you opened it to watch code run."
  (let (booting? (and (live?) (not (svc spec :running? '())))
        spy (get spec :spy))
    (do
      (when (live?) (svc spec :start (list m)))
      (let (existing (pg-index spec m))
        (if existing
          (pg-reselect spec (ed-select m existing))
          (message (workings/refresh spy
                     (ed-show-named m (buffer-name spec) (get spec :welcome) (get spec :type))
                     0
                     true)
            (open-message spec m booting?)))))))

(defn restart (spec m)
  "Restart this playground's session: a fresh child — everything the buffer defined is
gone — and the whole buffer re-evaluates against it once the child announces ready.

Also the retry path when the session gave up or never started, which is why it STARTS a dead
one rather than resetting it: a reset on a worker that is not running is a silent no-op, and
a silent no-op is the wrong answer to \"please try again\"."
  (do
    (when (live?) (if (svc spec :running? '()) (svc spec :reset '()) (svc spec :start (list m))))
    (message m (get spec :restart-message))))

;; ---- the mode line's count ---------------------------------------------------------

(defn broken-count (buffer)
  "How many of this buffer's forms cannot run yet — a syntax error (`:broken`) or a form
still being typed (`:incomplete`). Both are \"not ready\", and the difference between them
matters at the form and not in a count.

Not a form that is merely EVALUATING (`:running`) or HELD: both are ready, and counting them
would blink the chip on for the round trip of every send."
  (count (seq/filter (or (eolnotes/eol-notes-any buffer) {})
           (fn ([_ note]) (let (kind (:kind note)) (or (= kind :broken) (= kind :incomplete)))))))

(defn modeline (m buffer)
  "The playground's status-bar chip — `1 form not ready` — or `\"\"` when everything parses.

This is where a form that will not compile is reported, and it is reported HERE because of
where it is not: inline, in red, at every keystroke. A form is unparseable for most of the
characters it takes to write one, so a verdict at the form was a verdict about typing. The
note at the form still says which one (in the pending voice — dim, no colour), and the
count says how many, once, in the place a reader looks for the state of a buffer rather
than the state of a line."
  (let (n (broken-count buffer))
    (cond
      (= n 0) ""
      (= n 1) "1 form not ready"
      else (str n " forms not ready"))))
